<GuideLayout title="Intermediate 2.1 · Cash & Construction Backbone" current="intermediate">
  <div class="page-grid">
    <aside class="sidebar"><IntermediateNav current="2-1" /></aside>
    <article class="content">
      <header class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-kicker">Intermediate · Chapter 2.1</div>
        <h1 class="chapter-title">Cash & Construction Backbone</h1>
        <p class="chapter-summary">Chapter 1 did the slow preparation. This block turns its Earth orbs into spendable cash, then gives your house the tools to make the quest-heavy rest of Chapter 2 less of a travel chore. Work through it in a few comfortable sessions, changing activities whenever you want a break.</p>
      </header>

      <ChapterGoals
        summary="Use all 1,590 banked Earth orbs for battlestaves, alch the finished staves, train Construction from about 39 to 50, earn a plank sack through Mahogany Homes, reach at least base 62 for the lectern boost, and make an initial stack of 100 house teleport tablets. You can begin with the combat levels you already have."
        stats={["Crafting: +178,875 XP from all staves; check for 61+ before Lunar Diplomacy", "63 Magic → about 65 from 103,350 alchemy XP", "About 39 Construction → 50 for Adept contracts → 62 for the lectern boost", "350 Mahogany Homes points spent on the plank sack; 100 house tablets banked"]}
        unlocks={["The first large, repeatable cash injection for Chapter 2 supplies", "Adept Mahogany Homes and its 28-plank sack", "A mahogany eagle lectern and a practical house-teleport stock"]}
      />

      <div class="callout"><strong>Before you begin:</strong> have 1,590 charged Earth orbs, <strong>58 Crafting or enough mushroom pies to stay boosted while making the first staves</strong>, 63 Magic and about 39 Construction. Have the crystal saw ready before building the lectern later on this page; <a href="#i21-lectern-supplies">the preparation step</a> explains how to turn the reward seed into it. Your planted Fossil Island teaks will help with Construction later on this page. Check your remaining nature runes: Mage Training Arena used many of the 1,650 bought in 1.3. Also check your Construction level and carpenter points so you can buy only the materials still needed.</div>

      <section>
        <div class="section-label">The cash engine</div>
        <h2>Finally spend the Earth-orb stock.</h2>
        <p>We carried the orbs instead of selling them because each orb can become an <strong>Earth battlestaff</strong>: a solid Crafting XP drop, a High Alchemy cast, and cash to fund planks and house rooms. It is fine to split this over several days. Buy a manageable batch of plain battlestaves, attach orbs at a bank, then alch the products while doing something easy or while bankstanding.</p>
      </section>

      <RouteStep id="i21-spellbook-and-staves" number="1" title="Set up the guild shop, runes and first cash cycle">
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Return to the standard spellbook</strong> if Desert Treasure left you on Ancient Magicks. High Level Alchemy requires the standard spellbook; switch at the Desert Pyramid altar (or another spellbook switch you have unlocked).</li>
          <li>Go to the <strong>Wizards' Guild in Yanille</strong>. Entry needs <strong>66 Magic</strong>, but your 63 Magic plus a <strong>Wizard's mind bomb (+3)</strong> is enough. Drink immediately before entering; repeat for a later re-entry if the boost expires. The Guild rune-and-staff shop also requires <strong>The Hand in the Sand</strong>, finished immediately after Enlightened Journey in 1.4.</li>
          <li>Buy plain <strong>battlestaves</strong> from Wizard Akutha's rune-and-staff shop upstairs. It normally stocks five at about <strong>7,000 coins each</strong>; hop worlds or return as stock replenishes. Zaff in Varrock is a small supplementary source. Buying five at a time is repetitive: do this in manageable batches, not one uninterrupted 1,590-staff shop-hop/craft/alch session.</li>
          <li><strong>Check your remaining nature runes.</strong> You need one for each of the 1,590 finished staves, and Mage Training Arena used part of the 1,650 bought in 1.3. Buy any missing runes from this Guild shop (normally 250 in stock) or another accessible nature-rune source, in batches as the staff cycle returns cash. You can start before buying all 1,590. Equip a fire staff or supply fire runes.</li>
          <li><strong>Recheck law runes during these Guild visits.</strong> After funding the next manageable staff-and-nature batch, top up toward <strong>roughly 500 laws total</strong> as the alchs free up cash; subtract what remains from 1.4 instead of buying 500 automatically. This is a convenience stock for Construction travel and later quests, including <strong>100 laws for the house tablets below</strong>. Keep that tablet allocation separate and refill any travel shortfall; reaching 500 is not a condition for starting the staff cycle.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>If your coin pile is small, buy only a few staves, craft and alch them, then recycle the proceeds into the next batch. A finished Earth battlestaff alchs for <strong>9,300 coins</strong>; against a roughly 7,000-coin base staff, that is around <strong>2,300 coins returned above the staff cost</strong> before rune costs and shop-price variation. Across the full stock, the difference between staff purchase prices and alchemy returns is roughly <strong>3.6 million coins</strong>. Rune costs, shop prices and your other spending will reduce the cash left in your bank.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i21-attach-and-alch" number="2" title="Use mushroom pie until 58 Crafting, then finish without boosts">
        <p><strong>Earth battlestaves require 58 Crafting.</strong> Check your unboosted Crafting level. At 58+, attach the orbs without a pie. Below 58, use the mushroom pies banked at the end of 1.4; each bite boosts Crafting by +4. Making the Chapter 1 orbs and completing its quests brings you to at least 55, enough to boost to 58. If you are below 55, finish those earlier steps first. At a bank, use a charged Earth orb on a plain battlestaff and choose Make-All. Each attachment gives <strong>112.5 Crafting XP</strong>. Refresh the pie only while a boost is needed, and check your unboosted level after each batch.</p>
        <div class="callout"><strong>Stop using pie at 58 Crafting:</strong> as soon as your <em>unboosted</em> level reaches <strong>58</strong>, stop eating pie for this task. Continue attaching the remaining orbs without boosts. The full 1,590 attachments give <strong>178,875 Crafting XP</strong>; your finishing level depends on the XP you had before starting.</div>
        <p>High Alch the finished staves on the standard spellbook, using one nature rune per staff. All 1,590 casts award <strong>103,350 Magic XP</strong>. Alch during convenient downtime or simply stand at a bank. Starting at <strong>63 Magic</strong>, this batch takes you past <strong>65</strong>, needed for Lunar Diplomacy. If you started below 63 or alched fewer staves, check how much Magic training remains before that quest.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <TrainingOptions id="i21-alchemy-method" informational={true} skill="Staves + alchemy" target="all 1,590 orbs used" why="This converts the large Chapter 1 resource stock into immediate GP, Crafting XP and Magic XP before the costly house work." recommended="Buy manageable staff batches, attach orbs at the bank, then High Alch at your own pace. A few staves at a time is enough to begin; use each batch's alchemy proceeds to buy the next." relaxed="Bank the crafted staves and alch later during travel, waiting time, or a quiet bankstanding session. Set aside one nature rune for each staff still waiting to be alched, up to 1,590 for the full batch." fast="If you already enjoy Piscarilius artefacts, alch between movement actions for extra Thieving XP. This is optional; later Thieving steps work whether you try it or skip it." />

      <RouteStep id="i21-optional-artefacts" number="Optional" optional={true} title="Try Piscarilius artefacts only if you want active Thieving multiskilling">
        <ExpandableNote title="What is Piscarilius artefact stealing?" label="Activity primer">
          <p>At <strong>49 Thieving</strong>, talk to <strong>Captain Khaled</strong> in the east-side foodhall of Port Piscarilius. He assigns a house on the western docks. Bring a <strong>lockpick or hair clip</strong>, pick the assigned drawers, then walk the stolen artefact back past the patrols; teleporting while carrying it loses it. Hand it to Khaled for Thieving XP and another job. You can cast High Alchemy during the running portions if you like active multiskilling.</p>
          <p>Try this only if you enjoy combining two activities. You can skip it and alch at a bank instead. The Dorgesh-Kaan diary chest needs <strong>78 Thieving</strong>; leave that for later unless you already have the level.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>
      </RouteStep>

      <section>
        <div class="section-label">The house foundation</div>
        <h2>Turn the cash into lower-friction Construction.</h2>
        <p>Mahogany Homes is a contract loop: repair an NPC's house with planks, collect a completion reward, and take the next job. It uses fewer planks per XP than repetitive POH furniture building and lets us stop at useful unlocks. You already met it briefly in Chapter 1; here is the full routine.</p>
      </section>

      <TrainingOptions id="i21-construction-50" informational={true} skill="Construction" target="50, unboosted" why="Adept Mahogany Homes contracts unlock at unboosted level 50. Check your level after Chapter 1; if it is around 39, start with Novice contracts." recommended="Continue Novice Mahogany Homes contracts using oak planks until your unboosted Construction level is 50. Starting at level 39 this is about 37 average contracts, 375 oak planks and 15 steel bars. Prepare roughly 400–450 oak planks and 20 bars as a buffer; stop at 50 and save leftovers." relaxed="Break the novice jobs into short sets between Farming or birdhouse runs. Take whichever travel route is comfortable." fast="If you would rather finish this short bridge entirely in your POH, ordinary oak furniture/larders work; budget more oak planks because they lack contract-completion XP. The material estimates above are for Novice contracts." />

      <RouteStep id="i21-oak-prep" number="3" title="Prepare oak planks and finish Novice contracts to 50">
        <p>Check your bank first. Chop any missing <strong>oak logs</strong> normally, or use banked logs. The <strong>Woodcutting Guild</strong> has oak trees, a sawmill and a bank; your Chapter 1 Woodcutting level opens it. Ask the sawmill operator to turn each oak log into an oak plank for <strong>250 coins</strong>. A 400–450-plank budget costs about <strong>100,000–112,500 coins</strong> in conversion fees, plus any travel/steel-bar costs. Buy a saw if yours is missing; bring a hammer. Steel bars can be smelted from one iron ore and two coal each at your current Smithing level.</p>
        <p>Take <strong>Novice</strong> jobs from Amy just south of Falador park (or a Mahogany Homes contractor in Varrock, Hosidius or East Ardougne). Carry oak planks, a few steel bars, hammer, saw and ordinary city teleports. Go to the named client's home, repair or replace the marked furniture, speak to the homeowner for completion XP and points, then ask any contractor for the next job. No nails are needed. Stop when the skill tab shows <strong>50 Construction without a boost</strong>.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i21-teak-prep" number="4" title="Chop teaks normally and convert a practical plank stock">
        <p><strong>Before banking logs:</strong> check whether the <strong>Museum Camp bank chest</strong> was built during Bone Voyage. If not, build it now with <strong>21 Construction, a hammer, two oak planks, one iron bar and five nails</strong>. You now have the Construction level to build it.</p>
        <p>The two teaks planted in Chapter 1 occupy the <strong>two western hardwood patches in Fossil Island's Mushroom Forest</strong>. <strong>Check their health once for Farming XP, then leave them planted while gathering Construction logs</strong>; do not clear/replant them as part of the ordinary tree-run loop. Take a <strong>Digsite pendant</strong> to Fossil Island, use the mushtree network to <strong>Mushroom Meadow</strong>, then walk south-east to the hardwood patches. Bring an axe; chop normally, take each inventory back to the <strong>Museum Camp bank</strong> via the mushtree, and repeat. Ordinary chopping works well here. If your saplings are still growing—or you have not found both seeds from birdhouses—cut the accessible teak tree <strong>south-west of Castle Wars</strong> instead (ring of dueling to the bank, or fairy ring <strong>BKP</strong> nearby). Do not block the chapter waiting for a Farming timer.</p>
        <p>If you used the recommended <strong>Novice Mahogany Homes</strong> bridge, start with about <strong>900–1,000 teak logs/planks</strong> and <strong>40–50 steel bars</strong>, then check your carpenter points and Construction level before chopping more. If you trained 39→50 with <strong>POH furniture</strong> instead and have few or no carpenter points, the sack may take <strong>1,050+ teak planks</strong>; top up only as needed. From zero points, 88 full-value Adept jobs give 352 points and average about <strong>1,023 teak planks</strong> at 11.63 per job, before layout variance. Convert banked logs in normal inventory trips at the <strong>Woodcutting Guild sawmill</strong> for <strong>500 coins per teak plank</strong>, then use its bank. The first 900–1,000 planks cost roughly <strong>450,000–500,000 coins</strong> in sawmill fees. The Varrock Lumber Yard sawmill is an equally valid alternative. No butler or plank spell is required.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i21-adept-primer" number="5" title="Start Adept Mahogany Homes and learn the normal contract loop">
        <p>With <strong>unboosted 50 Construction</strong>, ask Amy or another Mahogany Homes contractor for an <strong>Adept</strong> job. These use <strong>teak planks</strong> instead of oak. Carry a hammer, saw, around 15 teak planks, one or two steel bars, and teleports for <strong>Falador, Varrock, Hosidius and East Ardougne</strong>. Kharedst's memoirs and your existing jewellery help with Kourend travel; walk the short remaining distance rather than trying to memorize a perfect teleport route.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Read the assigned homeowner and city in the contract dialogue, then travel to that house.</li>
          <li>Inside, click the damaged/highlighted furniture. Some pieces are <strong>repaired</strong>; others are removed and <strong>rebuilt</strong> with the teak planks. A damaged range or bathtub may consume a steel bar.</li>
          <li>Talk to the homeowner when all work is done. This awards the completion XP and <strong>four carpenter points</strong> for a full Adept contract. Ask any contractor for the next job; you do not need to return to Amy specifically.</li>
          <li>Bank more planks/bars when your inventory runs low. Repeat this loop at your own pace, using the teleports you have.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Your first goal is the <strong>plank sack</strong>. It costs <strong>350 carpenter points</strong> and holds up to <strong>28 planks</strong>, cutting down bank trips for future contracts and Construction. Check your current point balance first: Chapter 1's earlier Mahogany Homes jobs and this section's Novice bridge may already have earned points. <strong>From zero points, 88 full-value Adept jobs produce 352 points</strong> (88 × 4); with earlier points, fewer are needed. The mean per Adept job is about <strong>11.6 teak planks, 0.5 steel bars and 3,260 total Construction XP</strong>, with some variation between houses.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i21-plank-sack" number="6" title="Buy the plank sack and reach 62 Construction">
        <p>Continue Adept contracts until your <strong>total unspent points reach at least 350</strong>, then buy the plank sack from the Mahogany Homes reward shop. Use it on subsequent jobs if more XP is needed. Check your <strong>unboosted Construction level</strong>: the lectern plan needs <strong>at least 62</strong>. If the point grind leaves you below that, continue the same Adept loop to 62; if you reached 62 first, continue until you can buy the sack. Use your skill tab to see when you reach the level.</p>
        <div class="callout"><strong>How much training to expect:</strong> starting at level 50, reaching 62 takes about 232,500 XP, or roughly <strong>72 average Adept jobs</strong>. If you followed the Novice bridge, its points may bring the sack before 62. If you used POH furniture and have a low point balance, the sack may require more Adept jobs; 88 from zero points may place you above 62. Both outcomes are normal. Save any spare teak planks for the later training to 70 Construction in Chapter 2.</div>
      </RouteStep>

      <section>
        <div class="section-label">House travel</div>
        <h2>Build the lectern once; use it for the rest of the route.</h2>
        <p>The <strong>mahogany eagle lectern</strong> in a POH <strong>study</strong> makes standard house-teleport tablets. Those turn future quest preparation and contract travel into a predictable one-slot teleport instead of repeated spellbook/rune juggling. Its displayed build requirement is 67 Construction; you can build it at base 62 by combining tea and the crystal saw.</p>
      </section>

      <RouteStep id="i21-lectern-supplies" number="7" title="Prepare the study, kitchen and exact lectern materials">
        <p><strong>Check that you have the usable crystal saw.</strong> If you still have the <strong>crystal saw seed</strong> from The Eyes of Glouphrie, take it to <strong>Brimstail's cave in the south-west Tree Gnome Stronghold</strong>. From the Stronghold spirit tree, walk south-west to the dungeon entrance beside the small lake, enter the western chamber and <strong>use the seed on the singing bowl</strong>. This is free and needs no spell or runes. A depleted saw reverts to the seed; use the same bowl to restore it. Keep the saw in your inventory for the lectern build. <a href="https://oldschool.runescape.wiki/w/Crystal_saw" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Crystal saw and recharging</a>.</p>
        <p>Set aside a <strong>hammer, crystal saw, two mahogany planks and one gold leaf</strong> for the lectern. Cut two mahogany logs from the trees <strong>north of the Farming Guild</strong> (or use two already banked), then make the planks at a sawmill for <strong>1,500 coins each</strong>. Obtain the leaf from the <strong>Stonemason in south-west Keldagrim</strong> for about <strong>130,000 coins</strong>. Add a <strong>study</strong> in POH build mode (requires 40 Construction; 50,000 coins) and leave its lectern hotspot open. Room requirements cannot use the saw's invisible boost, but your unboosted 62 already covers this.</p>
        <p>For the reliable <strong>+2 tea</strong>, add a <strong>kitchen</strong> to the same house if you lack one (5,000 coins). Build or confirm: <strong>wooden shelves 3</strong> (three regular planks, six soft clay, three steel nails) or higher shelves that supply a <strong>porcelain cup</strong>; a <strong>wooden larder</strong> for tea leaves (eight regular planks and eight steel nails), or a better larder; a <strong>pump and drain</strong> (five steel bars), or better water source; and a <strong>firepit with hook</strong> (two steel bars, two soft clay), or a higher stove. A plain firepit is <em>not</em> enough to boil the kettle. If building the minimum setup from scratch, that is <strong>11 regular planks, 11 steel nails, eight soft clay and seven steel bars</strong>, plus the room fee. Check remaining soft clay and bracelet charges; the full tablet-and-kitchen budget is below. The porcelain cup determines the +2 boost.</p>
      
        <ExpandableNote label="Quest preparation" title="Replace missing clay and building materials">
          <p>A bracelet of clay is optional convenience. If none remains, <strong>mine ordinary clay at Rimmington and use water on it to make soft clay</strong>. Use banked <strong>vials of water</strong> at the bank if available: each clay uses one filled vial and returns an empty one; with a clear inventory, work in batches of 14 clay and 14 vials of water. Aemad in East Ardougne sells 100-vial water-filled packs if needed. Alternatively, refill a <strong>bucket</strong> at Rimmington's well after each clay; empty vials cannot be filled directly at a well. Make only enough to cover the missing kitchen and tablet clay.</p>
          <p>For missing regular planks, cut ordinary logs and pay <strong>100 coins per log at a sawmill</strong>; buy the needed nails and a saw there. For steel bars, buy iron ore and coal from <strong>Ordan in the Blast Furnace</strong> and smelt <strong>one iron ore plus two coal per bar</strong>. A hammer is sold at Lumbridge's general store.</p>
        </ExpandableNote>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i21-lectern-build" number="8" title="Make porcelain tea, apply both boosts and build immediately">
        <ol>
          <li>Enter your POH with the <strong>crystal saw in your inventory</strong>, a hammer, two mahogany planks and the gold leaf. Turn on <strong>build mode</strong> before the final build.</li>
          <li>From the kitchen shelves take a <strong>kettle, teapot and porcelain cup</strong>; get <strong>tea leaves</strong> from the larder. Use the kettle on the pump/sink to fill it, then put it on the firepit <em>with hook</em> or higher stove. Put leaves into the teapot. When the kettle boils, remove it and use the hot kettle on the teapot; pour a serving into the <em>porcelain</em> cup. Your Cooking level already exceeds the 20 needed to make tea.</li>
          <li>Drink the tea and check the skill tab: the <strong>visible level should show at least 64</strong> from base 62. The crystal saw adds another <strong>invisible +3</strong> only when building furniture that uses a saw. It does <em>not</em> appear in the skill tab, so confirm the saw is present and the lectern recipe is the 67-Construction furniture build.</li>
          <li>Go straight to the study's lectern hotspot and build the <strong>mahogany eagle lectern</strong>. If the tea boost decays below +2 before you click, drink another freshly made porcelain tea; if you cannot make the furniture, recheck the saw, hammer, build mode and materials. Refresh the tea when the visible boost falls below the level you need.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>If your Adept sessions carried you to unboosted 63 or higher, this exact setup still works; the extra level simply gives more timing slack. The permanent lectern is the payoff—the tea is only for construction, not for making tablets afterward.</p>
      </RouteStep>

      <RouteStep id="i21-house-tabs" number="9" title="Make and bank 100 house teleport tablets">
        <p>Use a round first stock of <strong>100 tablets</strong>. Bring <strong>100 soft clay, 100 law runes, 100 air runes and 100 earth runes</strong> to the finished lectern, in inventory batches. Each <strong>Teleport to House</strong> tablet requires <strong>40 Magic</strong> and one of each listed clay/rune. You can create it at the lectern <strong>regardless of your current spellbook</strong>; select it from the interface and repeat until the stack reaches 100. An elemental staff can replace the matching elemental runes if you prefer, but the listed rune budget works without one.</p>
        <p>If you chose to make <strong>bracelets of clay</strong> in Chapter 1, check how many charges remain. Each fresh bracelet has <strong>28 charges</strong> and makes mined clay arrive as soft clay. Check your banked soft clay and which kitchen fixtures are already built first: the maximum fresh need here is <strong>100 soft clay for tablets plus eight for the minimum kitchen</strong>. Four fresh bracelets cover 112 clay, but use only as many as your remaining shortfall needs. If a bracelet remains, equip it, mine clay and change bracelets only when its charges run out. <strong>If none remains, mine ordinary clay at Rimmington and soften it with banked vials of water or a refillable bucket</strong>, as described in the preparation note above. Check how many law runes remain after your Construction teleports. Buy the shortfall to <strong>100 laws for these tablets plus your chosen travel buffer</strong>, and any missing elemental runes, from an accessible rune shop such as the Wizards' Guild.</p>
        <div class="callout"><strong>When to refill:</strong> 100 tablets should cover many upcoming house visits. Make another batch when the stack gets low; no quest requires this exact starting quantity. Bank the tablets with the remaining cash and construction materials for 2.2.</div>
      </RouteStep>

      <section id="chapter-complete" class="completion-panel">
        <div class="section-label">2.1 exit check</div>
        <h3>You have cash, a plank sack and reliable house travel.</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>All <strong>1,590 Earth orbs</strong> have become battlestaves and been alched; bank the resulting cash. Check for at least 61 Crafting and 65 Magic before Lunar Diplomacy.</li>
          <li><strong>Unboosted 50+</strong> Construction unlocked Adept contracts; <strong>350 points</strong> bought the plank sack; <strong>unboosted 62+</strong> and the +5 setup built the mahogany eagle lectern. Your ending Construction level may be higher, depending on starting XP and contract layouts.</li>
          <li><strong>100 house teleport tablets</strong> are banked. Save unused oak/teak planks, steel bars and runes for the later quests and training to 70 Construction.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Next, use the new cash and house teleports to complete the quest chains in 2.2.</p>
      </section>

    </article>
